# Every form of every given name in this archive, and which language wrote it.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A question, asked plainly on 17 September 2026: «are you
# searching all the variations of a name?» For surnames the answer was yes and
# had been proved — ZUBRINICH returns 191 records and ZUBRINIC a different 119.
# For GIVEN names the answer was no. The FindMyPast target list handed the
# searcher whatever form the tree held, which in these parishes is usually the
# Latin of the baptismal entry, and tested against the index:
#
#     firstname=josip      returns records
#     firstname=joseph     returns records
#     firstname=josephus   0
#     firstname=giuseppe   0
#
# THE ARCHIVE HAD THE ANSWER ALREADY. tools/given_names.py has folded 197 forms
# into 29 groups since the first week, and nothing ever searched with them. What
# was missing was not the grouping but the LANGUAGE — because the question a
# searcher asks is not «what else was he called», it is «what would the clerk in
# THIS index have written», and that depends on who the clerk was working for.
#
# THE COLUMNS
#   canon     the group key, as tools/given_names.py folds it
#   form      the spelling
#   lang      latin · croatian · italian · german · hungarian · english · venetian
#   note      only where the form needs one
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES *NOT* DECIDE. Whether a form is ATTESTED — actually met in
# a record gathered here — is not a judgement and is not written down here. A
# script reads it out of the records and the build refuses if the marking
# drifts. That is the lesson of the sibling archive, whose own name page claimed
# every form came «from the forms that actually appear in these registers» when
# thirty-one per cent of them did not.
#
# LATIN IS ONE LANGUAGE WITH SIX ENDINGS. «Michaelem» is not another form of the
# name, it is Michael in the accusative because the priest wrote «baptizavi
# Michaelem». sources/../tools/latin_cases.py already knows this for 42 people;
# the case is carried here too, so the page can say so rather than listing the
# accusative as if it were a name anyone was called.
#
# HUNGARIAN IS IN THIS FILE AND ALMOST NOWHERE IN THE RECORDS. It is here on
# purpose. Senj was Zengg to the Hungarian press, Fiume was a corpus separatum
# of the Crown of St Stephen, and this archive already holds a KATALYN Zubrinich
# — Hungarian Katalin — without ever having searched a Hungarian index. Those
# rows are aids, and the page will mark them as aids.
#
# canon|form|lang|note|notNominative
#
# THE FIFTH COLUMN, added 20 September 2026 and renamed the next day. It was
# called `case`, and «abbreviated» is not a grammatical case, so the name lied
# about its values. It answers WHY THIS FORM IS NOT THE NOMINATIVE.
#
# It used to be DERIVED from
# `note`: if the note happened to read «genitive» it became the case, and if
# it read anything else the form had no case. So a form could carry a case OR
# a remark and never both — and in a Latin-register archive «genitive» AND
# «the form of arrival» are both worth saying about one word.
#
# Thirty-six rows had a case sitting in the note field. They have been moved
# across, leaving the note free. A four-column row still works exactly as it
# did, note-derivation included, because five sister archives are about to
# write tables of their own and none of them should have to migrate first.
#
# The vocabulary is accusative · genitive · dative or ablative · abbreviated,
# and the build refuses anything else. «abbreviated» is not a grammatical
# case and is in the list anyway, because what the column really answers is
# «why is this form not the nominative».
